{
  "_about": "Canonical deity tags for filtering and search. The free-text `deity` field stays exactly as sourced — it carries the epithet, the consort and the local name, which is the interesting part. This vocabulary sits beside it and answers the question a filter actually asks: which god or goddess is this temple for?",
  "_policy": "A tag is only ever DERIVED from text already in the record. Nothing here adds a fact: if `deity` does not name a figure, the record gets no tag rather than a guessed one. Aliases are matched case-insensitively on word boundaries against `deity`, `name` and `alt`.",
  "_generated_by": "scripts/build-deity-tags.mjs writes `deities` and `deityGroup` onto data/sites.json. Re-run it after any wave; never hand-edit those two fields.",
  "_note_on_suffixes": "Tamil and Sanskrit temple names encode the deity in the name itself — -eswarar/-esvara/-ishwar/-natha marks a Shiva temple, -perumal/-swamy a Vishnu one, -amman/-ambal a goddess. Those suffix rules recover the deity for hundreds of records whose `deity` string is only a local epithet.",
  "groups": {
    "Shaiva": "Shiva and his forms, family and attendants",
    "Vaishnava": "Vishnu and his avatars",
    "Shakta": "the Goddess in her forms",
    "Smarta": "pan-Hindu figures outside the three main streams",
    "Jain": "the Tirthankaras and Bahubali",
    "Buddhist": "Buddhas, bodhisattvas and relic shrines",
    "Sikh": "the Gurus and the Guru Granth Sahib"
  },
